{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/app at 58c804aa730baa6025fa3fd88c16b96bd4eba6e0: Gitseq's resident is bounded.\n\nThis is the half that decides whether back-pressure exists at all. internal/app constructed kernel.Options with SigningKey, CheckpointProfile and PreAppend and never set MaxQueueDepth, so it defaulted to zero, so the gate never ran, and every Gitseq resident stayed exactly as unbounded as before while the kernel tests passed and an artifact claimed the feature had shipped.\n\nThe resident now passes ResidentQueueDepth, a named constant of 32, counting the submission holding the sequencer lock. Zero survives in the kernel as the embedding opt-out; it is not a posture this application takes, and the constant says so where it is declared.\n\nTestResidentSequencerIsBounded reads the depth the resident actually constructed, not the kernel primitive and not the source text. Deleting the option from the construction fails it with \"resident sequencer is unbounded; the queue bound was not passed to the kernel\". I ran that mutation rather than assuming it: a test written to catch a regression I have already shipped once should be shown to catch it.\n\nThe test reaches the value through kernel.Submitter.QueueDepth, which is new, because package app cannot read an unexported kernel field. The alternative was to assert nothing about the shipped path, which is precisely how the gap shipped.\n\nThe tree here is byte-identical to the head that already passed the full gates; only the commit and its basis differ.\n\nThis rests on its authorizing request, my promise, and 82a33e46, the live artifact for what main carries at this path, which stays live until a guarded merge. Nothing here cites the earlier lineage: I retired an artifact that chain descended from, which staled it retroactively, so the history is named in prose and cited nowhere.","body":{"commit":"58c804aa730baa6025fa3fd88c16b96bd4eba6e0","path":"internal/app"}}